 ],
 "literal": "On the shore of the great ocean, the small white water-crow / disturbs the green leaves resembling the ears of a male elephant, / and searches in the cold backwaters at midnight. At that time, a lonely / chariot came and returned, they say. Because of that, / mother chides me. Are there not other / women with braided, loose hair and shining jewels, / who are young and innocent, / with mothers who do not harass them, who are indeed of good fortune?",
 "translation": "On the shore of the great ocean, a small white water-crow stirs the green leaves that look like elephant ears, searching the cold backwaters at midnight. They say a lonely chariot came and went then. Because of that, my mother chides me. Are there no other young, innocent women with braided, loose hair and shining jewels, who have mothers that do not harass them, and are thus truly fortunate?",
 "modern": "பெரிய கடல் கரையில் உள்ள சிறிய வெண்மையான நீர் காகம், ஆண் யானையின் காது போன்ற பசிய இலைகளைக் கலைத்து, நள்ளிரவில் குளிர்ந்த கழியில் இரையைத் தேடுகிறது. அந்த நேரத்தில் ஒரு தனிமையான தேர் வந்து சென்றதாக ஊரார் கூறுகிறார்கள். அதைக் கேட்டு என் தாய் என்னை வருத்துகிறாள். அவிழ்த்துவிட்ட பின்னல் கூந்தலையும் விளங்கும் அணிகலன்களையும் உடைய இளமையான, பேதைமை கொண்ட வேறு பெண்கள் இல்லையா? தங்களை வருத்தாத தாயைப் பெற்ற அவர்கள் நல்ல ஊழ்வினை உடையவர்கள் போலும்!",
 "explanation": "This poem is set in the neytal (coastal) landscape, where the heroine speaks to her close friend or to herself within hearing of her mother. She laments that her mother is chiding her based on rumors that the hero's chariot came to the coastal backwaters at midnight. The image of the small white water-crow stirring the elephant-ear-like green leaves in the dark backwaters to find food represents the hero coming secretly at night, disturbing her peaceful life and causing gossip. She enviously yet sadly points out that other young, innocent women in the village are fortunate to have understanding mothers who do not harass them, unlike her own mother who torments her over a mere rumor. Key words include kaḻi (backwater), tēr (chariot), annai (mother), and naṟpālōrē (those of good fortune).",
